40:{"type": "tool_result", "t": 1791232391.303, "agent": "evidence-researcher", "name": "search_research_evidence", "preview": "Found 6 hybrid matches from 'research_evidence':\nIMPORTANT: Cite Doc IDs when using this information.\n\n[Doc ID: PMID-24996200#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.032)\n  Title: Joint Commission requirements for discharge instructions in patients with heart failure: is understanding important for preventing readmissions?\n  Content: Research question: Joint Commission requirements for discharge instructions in patients with heart failure: is understanding important for preventing readmissions?\nAbstract: Many approaches have been considered to reduce heart failure (HF) readmissions. The Joint Commission on Accreditation of Health Care Organizations (JC) requires hospitals to provide patients admitted for HF with discharge instructions that address 6 topics related to HF management: diet, exercise, weight monitoring, worsenin...\n\n[Doc ID: PMID-27727296#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.032)\n  Title: The Intensity of Primary Care for Heart Failure Patients: A Determinant of Readmissions?\n  Content: Research question: The Intensity of Primary Care for Heart Failure Patients: A Determinant of Readmissions?\nAbstract: We aimed to classify patients with heart failure (HF) by the style of primary care they receive. We used the claim data ( Syst\u00e8me National d'Information Inter-R\u00e9gime de l'Assurance Maladie) of patients living in a French region. We evaluated three concepts. First, baseline clinical status with age and Charlson index. Second, primary care practice style with mean delay between con...\n\n[Doc ID: PMID-27601460#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.031)\n  Title: Do Acute Myocardial Infarction and Heart Failure Readmissions Flagged as Potentially Preventable by the 3M Potentially Preventable Readmissions Software Have Mo\n  Content: Research question: Do Acute Myocardial Infarction and Heart Failure Readmissions Flagged as Potentially Preventable by the 3M 