87:{"type": "delegate", "t": 1791232550.465, "agent": "lead", "subagent": "medication-safety", "request": "Review escalation 17 for synthetic Patient Y (SYN-Y) and propose or decline a medication change under policy CP-02. Escalation from care-coordinator: hold spironolactone due to hyperkalemia and reduced eGFR. Chart findings to use: 72F, HFrEF EF 30%; two HF readmissions in 90 days [SQL:patient]; on 2026-08-30 readmitted with weight +3.4 kg, potassium 5.6 mmol/L, eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2, sodium 133 while taking spironolactone, lisinopril, and potassium chloride; potassium chloride was held during stay [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011]. Potassium chloride was on 2026-09-04 discharge list despite held during stay [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012], and patient reported taking potassium tablet on 2026-09-15 [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013], but clinician subsequently approved and executed holding potassium chloride. Other current concerns: patient reported +3.1 kg, new ankle swelling, and two pillows on 2026-09-15 [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]. Research/reference available: damaged kidneys can cause potassium buildup and serious heart problems [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0]; CKD can cause potassium to rise to unsafe levels [MEDQUAD-02311#chunk-0]; a spironolactone safety study still withdrew one patient for severe hyperkalemia, supporting careful monitor ..."}
