102:{"type": "tool", "t": 1791232642.98, "agent": "lead", "name": "PreVisitBrief", "args": "{\"snapshot\": \"Patient Y is a 72-year-old woman with HFrEF, EF 30%, coming for a post-discharge heart-failure clinic visit after two heart-failure readmissions in the last 90 days. [SQL:patient] The readmissions appear driven by recurrent congestion plus medication-access and adherence barriers: she stopped the afternoon furosemide before the 2026-07-19 readmission, later picked up furosemide 9 days late, and reported medication copay strain on a fixed income. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] The most concerning current safety issue is hyperkalemia risk in the setting of K 5.6 mmol/L and eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2 while she was taking spironolactone, lisinopril, and potassium chloride. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] The clinic should first determine whether she is actively decompensated because she reported 3.1 kg weight gain, new ankle swelling, and two-pillow sleep after discharge. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]\", \"what_changed\": [{\"headline\": \"Congestion/readmission pattern\", \"detail\": \"The patient\\u2019s heart-failure course escalated from a 2026-06-03 admission for dyspnea and leg edema with BNP 980 pg/mL to a 2026-07-19 readmission 41 days after discharge with orthopnea after she stopped the afternoon furosemide dose to avoid frequent urination. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0008] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] She then had another readmission on 2026-08-30 with weight 3.4 kg above discharge, which makes recurrent volume overload the central readmission pattern. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011]\"}, {\"headline\": \"Current symptom burden\", \"detail\": \"After the 2026-08-30 readmission, she reported on 2026-09-15 that her weight was 3.1 kg above discharge weight, with new ankle swelling and needing two pillows to sleep. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] These symptoms are concerning because heart-failure reference material identifies dyspnea/trouble breathing and ankle, foot, or leg swelling as common fluid-overload manifestations. [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-0]\"}, {\"headline\": \"Potassium and kidney safety\", \"detail\": \"The latest charted hospital values were potassium 5.6 mmol/L, eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2, and sodium 133 mmol/L on 2026-08-30 while she was taking spironolactone, lisinopril, and potassium chloride. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] The high-risk combination matters because kidney impairment can allow potassium to build up to dangerous levels and because CKD can cause potassium to rise to unsafe levels. [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0] [MEDQUAD-02311#chunk-0]\"}, {\"headline\": \"Access barriers remain unresolved\", \"detail\": \"She picked up furosemide 9 days late on 2026-07-30 and reported difficulty affording copays for eight medicines on a fixed income. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] She declined home-health nursing because of cost on 2026-09-04 and later requested transport assistance on 2026-09-15. [SQL:referral] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]\"}, {\"headline\": \"Heart-failure follow-up is scheduled but must be actionable\", \"detail\": \"The chart shows a heart-failure clinic appointment scheduled for 2026-09-18 and another heart-failure clinic visit requested for 2026-10-12. [SQL:appointment] Because the recurrent admissions are linked to missed or modified diuretic use and access barriers, the visit needs a medication-access and volume-status plan rather than routine follow-up alone. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010]\"}], \"medications_to_review\": [{\"medication\": \"Furosemide 40 mg twice daily\", \"concern\": \"Nonadherence and delayed refill driving congestion\", \"chart_evidence\": \"She was discharged on furosemide 40 mg twice daily on 2026-06-08 and later stopped the afternoon dose before the 2026-07-19 readmission. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0008] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] A 9-day late furosemide refill was documented on 2026-07-30, alongside copay difficulty for eight medications. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010]\", \"sources\": \"[SYN-Y-NOTE-0008] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010]\"}, {\"medication\": \"Spironolactone, dose not specified in chart-analyst report\", \"concern\": \"Hyperkalemia risk with ACE inhibitor and reduced eGFR\", \"chart_evidence\": \"She was taking spironolactone with lisinopril and potassium chlori ..."}
