107:{"type": "brief", "t": 1791232647.879, "patient": "SYN-Y", "markdown": "# Pre-visit brief - Patient Y (SYN-Y)\n> SYNTHETIC RECORD - invented for a demo, not a real patient. For clinician review; not medical advice.\n\n## Snapshot\nPatient Y is a 72-year-old woman with HFrEF, EF 30%, coming for a post-discharge heart-failure clinic visit after two heart-failure readmissions in the last 90 days. [SQL:patient] The readmissions appear driven by recurrent congestion plus medication-access and adherence barriers: she stopped the afternoon furosemide before the 2026-07-19 readmission, later picked up furosemide 9 days late, and reported medication copay strain on a fixed income. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] The most concerning current safety issue is hyperkalemia risk in the setting of K 5.6 mmol/L and eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2 while she was taking spironolactone, lisinopril, and potassium chloride. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] The clinic should first determine whether she is actively decompensated because she reported 3.1 kg weight gain, new ankle swelling, and two-pillow sleep after discharge. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]\n\n## What changed\n- **Congestion/readmission pattern.** The patient\u2019s heart-failure course escalated from a 2026-06-03 admission for dyspnea and leg edema with BNP 980 pg/mL to a 2026-07-19 readmission 41 days after discharge with orthopnea after she stopped the afternoon furosemide dose to avoid frequent urination. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0008] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] She then had another readmission on 2026-08-30 with weight 3.4 kg above discharge, which makes recurrent volume overload the central readmission pattern. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011]\n- **Current symptom burden.** After the 2026-08-30 readmission, she reported on 2026-09-15 that her weight was 3.1 kg above discharge weight, with new ankle swelling and needing two pillows to sleep. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] These symptoms are concerning because heart-failure reference material identifies dyspnea/trouble breathing and ankle, foot, or leg swelling as common fluid-overload manifestations. [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-0]\n- **Potassium and kidney safety.** The latest charted hospital values were potassium 5.6 mmol/L, eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2, and sodium 133 mmol/L on 2026-08-30 while she was taking spironolactone, lisinopril, and potassium chloride. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] The high-risk combination matters because kidney impairment can allow potassium to build up to dangerous levels and because CKD can cause potassium to rise to unsafe levels. [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0] [MEDQUAD-02311#chunk-0]\n- **Access barriers remain unresolved.** She picked up furosemide 9 days late on 2026-07-30 and reported difficulty affording copays for eight medicines on a fixed income. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] She declined home-health nursing because of cost on 2026-09-04 and later requested transport assistance on 2026-09-15. [SQL:referral] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]\n- **Heart-failure follow-up is scheduled but must be actionable.** The chart shows a heart-failure clinic appointment scheduled for 2026-09-18 and another heart-failure clinic visit requested for 2026-10-12. [SQL:appointment] Because the recurrent admissions are linked to missed or modified diuretic use and access barriers, the visit needs a medication-access and volume-status plan rather than routine follow-up alone. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010]\n\n## Medications to review\n| Medication | Concern | What the chart shows | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Furosemide 40 mg twice daily | Nonadherence and delayed refill driving congestion | She was discharged on furosemide 40 mg twice daily on 2026-06-08 and later stopped the afternoon dose before the 2026-07-19 readmission. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0008] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] A 9-day late furosemide refill was documented on 2026-07-30, alongside copay difficulty for eight medications. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] | [SYN-Y-NOTE-0008] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] |\n| Spironolactone, dose not specified in chart-analyst report | Hyperkalemia risk with ACE inhibitor and reduced eGFR | She was taking spironolactone with lisinopril and potassium chloride when she was readmitted on 2026-08-30 with potassium 5.6 mmol/L and eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] Medication-safety proposed holding spironolactone through escalation 17, action 134, pending physician decision. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] | [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] |\n| Lisinopril, dose not specified in chart-analyst report | Contributes to hyperkalemia risk but is disease-modifying therapy | Lisinopril was being taken with spironolactone and potassium chloride at the time of the 2026-08-30 readmission with potassium 5.6 mmol/L and eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] The clinician should reassess renin-angiotensin system therapy after repeat potassium and renal status are known, because ACE inhibitors are among heart-failure medications listed in reference material. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [MEDQUAD-03657#chunk-0] | [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [MEDQUAD-03657#chunk-0] |\n| Potassium chloride, dose not specified in current chart-analyst report | Recent inappropriate continuation after hyperkalemia | Potassium chloride was held during the 2026-08-30 hospitalization but was still on the 2026-09-04 discharge medication list, and the patient reported taking the potassium tablet on 2026-09-15. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] The current memory states the clinician later approved holding potassium chloride, so the visit should verify that she actually stopped it and removed it from home supply. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] | [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] |\n| SGLT2 inhibitor not documented | Potential HFrEF GDMT gap after stabilization | The discharge documentation did not document an SGLT2 inhibitor or a reason for omission. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012] This gap should be revisited after potassium, renal function, and volume status are stabilized. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] | [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012] |\n\nThe key interaction concern is the combination of spironolactone, lisinopril, and potassium supplementation in a patient who developed potassium 5.6 mmol/L with eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] This is clinically important because kidney damage can cause potassium buildup with serious heart problems, and CKD can allow potassium to rise to unsafe levels. [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0] [MEDQUAD-02311#chunk-0]\n\n## Overdue monitoring and open care gaps\n- **Active decompensation assessment.** She reported 3.1 kg weight gain, new ankle swelling, and two-pillow sleep on 2026-09-15 after recent discharge. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] Heart-failure reference material identifies shortness of breath or trouble breathing and swelling in ankles, feet, or legs as common fluid-overload signs that can require urgent evaluation. [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-0] [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-2]\n- **Medication access and adherence plan.** The July readmission followed self-stopping the afternoon furosemide dose, and the later refill was picked up 9 days late. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] The patient also reported difficulty with copays for eight medicines on a fixed income, making access a likely readmission driver. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010]\n- **Home monitoring and support alternatives.** The patient initially lacked a home scale before the 2026-07-19 readmission and then received a scale at discharge. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] She declined home-health nursing due to cost, and transport assistance was requested. [SQL:referral] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]\n- **Hyperkalemia follow-through.** Potassium chloride was held during the 2026-08-30 stay but reappeared on the discharge list, and she later reported taking the potassium tablet. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] The visit should confirm potassium chloride discontinuation, review all bottles, and reconcile spironolactone and lisinopril against current labs. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011]\n- **GDMT optimization after stabilization.** The chart analyst found no SGLT2 inhibitor documented and no reason for omission. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012] Heart-failure reference material lists multiple medication classes for management, including diuretics, ACE inhibitors, beta blockers, and digoxin, so the clinic should review whether disease-modifying therapy is complete after safety issues are controlled. [MEDQUAD-03657#chunk-0]\n\n## What the reference and research say\n### Clinical reference\n- Heart-failure reference material states that common signs and symptoms of heart failure include shortness of breath or trouble breathing, fatigue, and swelling in the ankles, feet, legs, abdomen, or neck veins. [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-0] The same reference states that the presence of these heart-failure symptoms can require emergency treatment. [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-2] *This directly supports prioritizing volume-status assessment because she reported weight gain, ankle swelling, and two-pillow sleep after discharge. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]*\n- Clinical reference material recommends preventing fluid buildup by maintaining a healthy weight and following a low-salt diet because salt can cause extra fluid to build up in the body. [MEDQUAD-03651#chunk-0] Another reference states that too much sodium causes blood to hold fluid and strains the heart and kidneys. [MEDQUAD-01694#chunk-0] *This supports addressing sodium intake and weight trends as part of the readmission-prevention plan. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]*\n- The heart-failure medication reference lists diuretics among medicines used to manage heart failure and states that diuretics reduce fluid buildup. [MEDQUAD-03657#chunk-0] The same source lists ACE inhibitors and beta blockers among medications used to manage heart failure. [MEDQUAD-03657#chunk-0] *This supports focusing on furosemide adherence first, while later reassessing disease-modifying therapy once labs and congestion are stabilized. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011]*\n- Kidney-disease reference material states that damaged kidneys can cause potassium buildup, which can lead to serious heart problems. [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0] CKD reference material also states that potassium can rise to unsafe levels in chronic kidney disease. [MEDQUAD-02311#chunk-0] *This supports urgent medication reconciliation and repeat renal/electrolyte review after potassium 5.6 mmol/L with eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011]*\n\n### Research evidence\n- A study of signs and symptoms of heart failure found that hospitalized patients reported more sudden weight gain, and sudden weight gain increased from 5% in functional class I to 37% in functional class IV. [PMID-19940253] This supports sudden weight gain as a marker of worsening heart-failure severity. [PMID-19940253] *This heightens concern about her reported 3.1 kg post-discharge weight gain. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]*\n- A BNP-guided chronic heart-failure management analysis found lower readmission and mortality with BNP-guided management and lower costs compared with clinical management. [PMID-15262031] This supports objective reassessment of congestion when symptoms and weight suggest worsening heart failure. [PMID-15262031] *This is relevant because she had BNP 980 pg/mL during the June admission and recurrent post-discharge congestion. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0008] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]*\n- A cost-effectiveness study comparing home-based and clinic-based chronic heart-failure management in elderly patients found that home-based intervention was potentially cost-effective over the long term. [PMID-27841726] This supports revisiting affordable home-based monitoring or alternatives when clinic access is difficult. [PMID-27841726] *This matters because she declined home-health nursing due to cost and requested transport assistance. [SQL:referral] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]*\n- A study on discharge instructions in heart-failure patients noted that preventing readmissions has driven many approaches and that understanding discharge instructions is important for readmission prevention. [PMID-24996200] This supports medication reconciliation and practical adherence planning after discharge. [PMID-24996200] *This is relevant because she stopped an afternoon furosemide dose and later had a delayed refill. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010]*\n- A spironolactone safety study in hemodialysis patients with baseline potassium below 5.6 mEq/L found no significant mean potassium rise over 28 days, but one patient was withdrawn for severe hyperkalemia. [PMID-14551367] This supports careful potassium monitoring whenever spironolactone is used in kidney impairment. [PMID-14551367] *This is relevant because she had potassium 5.6 mmol/L and eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2 while taking spironolactone and lisinopril. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011]*\n\n## Insights for the clinician: next steps\n1. **Assess for active decompensated heart failure first.** Her 3.1 kg post-discharge weight gain, new ankle swelling, and two-pillow sleep make current volume overload the first clinical question. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] Reference material identifies dyspnea/trouble breathing and ankle or leg swelling as common heart-failure fluid-overload signs that can require urgent evaluation. [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-0] [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-2]\n2. **Review urgent labs before adjusting chronic GDMT.** Potassium 5.6 mmol/L, eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2, and sodium 133 mmol/L occurred while she was taking spironolactone, lisinopril, and potassium chloride. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] Kidney impairment can allow potassium to build up to unsafe levels with serious heart risks. [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0] [MEDQUAD-02311#chunk-0]\n3. **Have the doctor decide on the spironolactone hold.** Medication-safety resolved escalation 17 by proposing action 134 to hold spironolactone, and this medication change waits for the doctor under policy CP-02. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] Spironolactone use requires careful potassium monitoring in kidney impairment because hyperkalemia can occur. [PMID-14551367]\n4. **Rebuild the readmission plan around practical diuretic adherence.** The July readmission followed stopping the afternoon furosemide dose, and a later furosemide refill was 9 days late. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] Diuretics reduce fluid buildup, so the visit should solve the toileting, timing, refill, and copay barriers that prevent use. [MEDQUAD-03657#chunk-0]\n5. **Address cost, transport, and home-support barriers during the visit.** She reported fixed-income medication copay difficulty, declined home-health nursing because of cost, and requested transport assistance. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] [SQL:referral] Home-based heart-failure intervention can be cost-effective in elderly heart-failure patients, so lower-cost monitoring alternatives should be explored. [PMID-27841726]\n6. **Optimize HFrEF therapy only after stabilization.** No SGLT2 inhibitor or reason for omission was documented. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012] The immediate priorities are volume status and potassium/renal safety, then reassessment of disease-modifying HFrEF therapy. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011]\n\n## Proposed actions (awaiting clinician approval)\n- **#132 \u00b7 lab request.** Request urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio to assess albuminuria in CKD and refine renal-risk assessment. [SQL:referral]\n- **#133 \u00b7 patient message.** Send a logistics/access-focused message about appointment support, medication-cost help, and care-access coordination. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] [SQL:referral]\n- **#131 \u00b7 follow up.** Arrange urgent follow-up for worsening heart-failure symptoms and recent post-discharge congestion. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]\n- **#134 \u00b7 medication change.** Medication-safety proposes holding spironolactone for hyperkalemia risk with K 5.6 mmol/L and eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2, and this awaits the doctor under policy CP-02. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011]\n\n## Questions for the visit\n1. Are weight, edema, orthopnea, dyspnea, blood pressure, oxygenation, and exam findings consistent with active decompensated heart failure today? [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]\n2. Did she stop potassium chloride after the physician order, and are any potassium tablets still in the home medication supply? [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]\n3. What are today\u2019s potassium, creatinine/eGFR, sodium, magnesium, and BNP results, and do they support holding spironolactone or changing diuretic intensity? [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011]\n4. Why did she stop the afternoon furosemide dose, and can timing, toileting plan, refill synchronization, or cost support make twice-daily dosing feasible? [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010]\n5. Can transport assistance and lower-cost home-based monitoring be arranged after she declined home-health nursing because of cost? [SQL:referral] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]\n6. After potassium and volume status are stabilized, is there a documented reason she is not on an SGLT2 inhibitor or other optimized HFrEF therapy? [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012]\n7. Does she understand the discharge plan well enough to explain what each heart-failure medication is for and what to do if a dose is difficult to take? [PMID-24996200]\n\n## Sources\n- [SQL:patient] Patient table: age, post-discharge HF visit context, and two HF readmissions in 90 days.\n- [SQL:appointment] Appointment table: HF clinic appointment scheduled 2026-09-18 and HF clinic visit requested 2026-10-12.\n- [SQL:referral] Referral table: home-health nursing declined due to cost and transport assistance requested.\n- [SYN-Y-NOTE-0008] 2026-06-03 heart-failure admission note: dyspnea, leg edema, BNP 980, EF 30%, discharge on furosemide 40 mg twice daily.\n- [SYN-Y-NOTE-0009] 2026-07-19 readmission note: orthopnea after stopping afternoon furosemide, no home scale, scale provided at discharge.\n- [SYN-Y-NOTE-0010] 2026-07-30 post-discharge access note: furosemide refill 9 days late and medication copay difficulty.\n- [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] 2026-08-30 readmission note: weight gain, potassium 5.6, eGFR 42, sodium 133 while taking spironolactone, lisinopril, and potassium chloride.\n- [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012] 2026-09-04 discharge note: potassium chloride on discharge list, home health declined due to cost, no SGLT2 inhibitor documented.\n- [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013] 2026-09-15 follow-up note: potassium tablet use, 3.1 kg weight gain, ankle swelling, two-pillow sleep, transport assistance requested.\n- [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-0] Heart failure signs and symptoms: dyspnea, fatigue, and swelling.\n- [MEDQUAD-04254#chunk-2] Heart failure symptoms requiring emergency treatment.\n- [MEDQUAD-03651#chunk-0] Heart-failure prevention: healthy weight and low-salt diet to prevent fluid buildup.\n- [MEDQUAD-01694#chunk-0] Sodium and fluid retention: excess sodium strains heart and kidneys.\n- [MEDQUAD-03657#chunk-0] Heart-failure medicines: diuretics, ACE inhibitors, beta blockers, and digoxin.\n- [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0] Kidney disease and potassium buildup causing serious heart problems.\n- [MEDQUAD-02311#chunk-0] Chronic kidney disease: potassium can rise to unsafe levels.\n- [PMID-19940253] Signs and symptoms of heart failure: are you asking the right questions?\n- [PMID-15262031] B-type natriuretic peptide-guided heart failure management: a cost-effectiveness analysis.\n- [PMID-27841726] Long-term cost-effectiveness of home versus clinic-based management of chronic heart failure: the WHICH? study.\n- [PMID-24996200] Joint Commission requirements for discharge instructions in patients with heart failure: is understanding important for preventing readmissions?\n- [PMID-14551367] Safety of spironolactone in dialysis patients.\n"}

