119:{"type": "delegate", "t": 1791233005.445, "agent": "lead", "subagent": "medication-safety", "request": "Review and formally resolve medication-safety escalation #18 for Patient Y under policy CP-02/CP-03. Escalation concern: hold lisinopril due to hyperkalemia. Patient context from chart analyst: female born 1954 with HFrEF EF 30%, T2DM, CKD/eGFR latest 42 mL/min/1.73m2 on 2026-08-30 down from 51 on 2026-06-03 and 46 on 2026-07-19; latest K 5.6 mmol/L, Na 133, BNP 1410. Active medications: lisinopril 10 mg daily, spironolactone 25 mg daily, metformin 500 mg twice daily, furosemide 40 mg twice daily, carvedilol 6.25 mg twice daily, aspirin 81 mg daily, atorvastatin 80 mg daily. Patient has recurrent HF readmissions and current congestion symptoms on 2026-09-15: new ankle swelling, two pillows, weight 73.9 kg, 3.1 kg above discharge. Prior clinician-approved potassium chloride hold executed; do not repeat potassium chloride hold. Prior spironolactone hold was proposed in previous briefs but no clinician decision is documented in memory; avoid duplicating if possible, but if spironolactone is the safer medication concern than lisinopril, explain. Research: CKD can cause potassium buildup and serious heart problems [MEDQUAD-01695]; hyperkalemia in HF/CKD on RAASi often causes therapy dis ..."}
