123:{"type": "tool_result", "t": 1791233008.788, "agent": "medication-safety", "name": "list_escalations", "preview": "#18 [open] from care-coordinator: Hold lisinopril due to hyperkalemia\n  reason: Patient's potassium is 5.6 [SQL:lab_result]. Lisinopril can increase potassium, exacerbating hyperkalemia. Holding lisinopril is proposed to help manage this.\n  citations: [SQL:lab_result]\n#17 [accepted] from care-coordinator: Hold spironolactone due to hyperkalemia and reduced eGFR\n  reason: Patient experienced hyperkalemia (K 5.6) and reduced eGFR (42) while on spironolactone and lisinopril, and reported potassium tablet use. Holding spironolactone is crucial to prevent further hyperkalemia and acute kidney injury. [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]\n  citations: [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0013]\n#12 [accepted] from care-coordinator: Hold Spironolactone: K 5.6 and eGFR 42 on spironolactone\n  reason: Patient has HFrEF, CKD 3a, and T2DM. Latest labs show potassium 5.6 and eGFR 42. eGFR has been rapidly declining. Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic, and its continued use in the setting of hyperkalemia and worsening renal function poses a significant safety concern. I proposed to hold Spironolactone.\n  citations: [SQL:lab_result] [SQL:condition] [SQL:cohort_benchmark] [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0] [PMID-11500745]\n#10 [accepted] from care-coordinator: Hold potassium chloride: K 5.6 on spironolactone\n  reason: Patient SYN-Y has active potassium chloride 20 mEq daily. On 2026-08-30, K was 5.6 mmol/L and eGFR 42. The patient is also on lisinopril and spironolactone. Hyperkalemia was noted during admission, and KCl remained on the discharge list. The requested medication-safety question is to hold potassium chloride pending urgent repeat BMP and clinician review, and for the doctor to reassess lisinopril/spironolactone if potassium remains elevated.\n  citations: [SQL:medication] [SQL:lab_result] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0011] [SYN-Y-NOTE-0012] [MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0] [PMID-14551367]"}
