132:{"type": "tool_result", "t": 1791233015.973, "agent": "medication-safety", "name": "query_chart", "preview": "{'note_date': '2026-09-15', 'body': 'Patient reports weight 73.9 kg on her home scale this morning (3.1 kg above discharge weight), new ankle swelling and needing two pillows to sleep. Reports taking all medicines including the potassium tablet. Advised to attend clinic; she asked whether transport can be arranged.', 'note_ref': 'SYN-Y-NOTE-0013'}\n{'note_date': '2026-09-04', 'body': 'Discharge medication list reconciled by the night team; potassium chloride appears on the discharge list again. No SGLT2 inhibitor; reason not documented. Follow-up in heart failure clinic within 14 days requested. Home health not arranged - patient declined because of cost.', 'note_ref': 'SYN-Y-NOTE-0012'}\n{'note_date': '2026-08-30', 'body': 'Second readmission in 90 days. Weight up 3.4 kg from discharge. Potassium 5.6 on admission while taking spironolactone, lisinopril and potassium chloride together. Potassium chloride held during stay. eGFR 42. Sodium 133.', 'note_ref': 'SYN-Y-NOTE-0011'}\n{'note_date': '2026-07-30', 'body': 'Patient asked about cost of her medications; copay for eight medicines is difficult on a fixed income. Furosemide refill picked up 9 days late.', 'note_ref': 'SYN-Y-NOTE-0010'}\n{'note_date': '2026-07-19', 'body': \"Readmitted 41 days after discharge with orthopnoea. Patient explains she stopped the afternoon furosemide dose 'to avoid running to the bathroom' on days she takes the bus to the shops. No home scale. Diuresed, discharged 2026-07-24 with a scale provided by social work.\", 'note_ref': 'SYN-Y-NOTE-0009'}\n{'note_date': '2026-06-03', 'body': 'Admitted with dyspnoea and leg oedema. BNP 980. Diuresed with IV furosemide, 3.1 kg net loss. Echo EF 30%, unchanged. Discharged 2026-06-08 on furosemide 40 mg twice daily with a daily-weights instruction sheet.', 'note_ref': 'SYN-Y-NOTE-0008'}"}
133:{"type": "tool_result", "t": 1791233016.041, "agent": "medication-safety", "name": "search_clinical_reference", "preview": "Found 6 hybrid matches from 'clinical_reference':\nIMPORTANT: Cite Doc IDs when using this information.\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-01412#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.033)\n  Title: What are the complications of Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease ?\n  Content: Question: What are the complications of Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease ?\nAnswer: Heart problems are a complication of anemia and may include-an irregular heartbeat or an unusually fast heartbeat, especially when exercising.-the harmful enlargement of muscles in the heart.-heart failure, which does not mean the heart suddenly stops working. Instead, heart failure is a long-lasting condition in which the heart cant pump enough blood to meet the bodys needs.\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-04252#chunk-2] (relevance: 0.030)\n  Title: What causes Heart Failure ?\n  Content: Blood pressure is considered high if it stays at or above 140/90 mmHg over time. (The mmHg is millimeters of mercurythe units used to measure blood pressure.) If you have diabetes or chronic kidney disease, high blood pressure is defined as 130/80 mmHg or higher.\n\nOther Heart Conditions or Diseases\n\nOther conditions and diseases also can lead to heart failure, such as:\n\nArrhythmia. Happens when a problem occurs with the rate or rhythm of the heartbeat.\n\nCardiomyopathy.Happens when the heart musc...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-02311#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.030)\n  Title: What is (are) Nutrition for Early Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\n  Content: Question: What is (are) Nutrition for Early Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\nAnswer: CKD usually takes a long time to develop and does not go away. In CKD, the kidneys continue to workjust not as well as they should. Wastes may build up so gradually that the body becomes used to having those wastes in the blood. Salts containing phosphorus and potassium may rise to unsafe levels, causing heart and bone problems. Anemialow red blood cell countcan result from CKD because the kidneys stop making ...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-01688#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.030)\n  Title: What is (are) Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\n  Content: Question: What is (are) Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\nAnswer: CKD usually takes a long time to develop and does not go away. In CKD, the kidneys continue to workjust not as well as they should. Wastes may build up so gradually that the body becomes used to having those wastes in the blood. Salts containing phosphorus and potassium may rise to unsafe levels, causing heart and bone problems. Anemialow red blood cell countcan result from CKD because the kidneys stop maki...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-05638#chunk-1] (relevance: 0.016)\n  Title: What is (are) hyperkalemic periodic paralysis ?\n  Content: Most people with hyperkalemic periodic paralysis have increased levels of potassium in their blood (hyperkalemia) during attacks. Hyperkalemia results when the weak or paralyzed muscles release potassium ions into the bloodstream. In other cases, attacks are associated with normal ..."}
134:{"type": "thought", "t": 1791233024.919, "agent": "medication-safety", "text": "Escalation #18:\nDecision: Proposed medication change.\nMedication: Lisinopril\nChange: hold\nReason: Patient SYN-Y has hyperkalemia (Potassium 5.6 mmol/L on 2026-08-30) and chronic kidney disease with worsening eGFR (42 mL/min/1.73m2 on 2026-08-30) [SQL:lab_result]. Lisinopril, an ACE inhibitor, is a RAAS inhibitor that can contribute to hyperkalemia, particularly in patients with impaired renal function [SQL:medication], [MEDQUAD-01695]. Hyperkalemia in patients with heart failure and chronic kidney disease on RAAS inhibitors often necessitates therapy discontinuation [PMID-31809752, PMID-30481200]. Holding lisinopril is a critical intervention to manage the current hyperkalemia and mitigate further risks.\nCitations: [SQL:lab_result], [SQL:medication], [MEDQUAD-01695], [PMID-31809752], [PMID-30481200]\n\nThis proposed medication change will be sent to the doctor for approval, as per policy CP-02."}
135:{"type": "tool", "t": 1791233024.919, "agent": "medication-safety", "name": "propose_medication_change", "args": "{\"citations\": \"[SQL:lab_result], [SQL:medication], [MEDQUAD-01695], [PMID-31809752], [PMID-30481200]\", \"escalation_id\": 18, \"change\": \"hold\", \"reason\": \"Patient SYN-Y has hyperkalemia (Potassium 5.6 mmol/L on 2026-08-30) and chronic kidney disease with worsening eGFR (42 mL/min/1.73m2 on 2026-08-30) [SQL:lab_result]. Lisinopril, an ACE inhibitor, is a RAAS inhibitor that can contribute to hyperkalemia, particularly in patients with impaired renal function [SQL:medication], [MEDQUAD-01695]. Hyperkalemia in patients with heart failure and chronic kidney disease on RAAS inhibitors often necessitates therapy discontinuation [PMID-31809752, PMID-30481200]. Holding lisinopril is a critical intervention to manage the current hyperkalemia and mitigate further risks.\", \"medication\": \"Lisinopril\"}"}
