{"type": "thought", "t": 1791233178.448, "agent": "guideline-researcher", "text": "Here are the clinical reference findings for your questions:\n\n*   **MEDQUAD-03643**: Worsening heart failure symptoms include shortness of breath, fatigue, and swelling in the ankles, feet, legs, and abdomen due to fluid buildup. This fluid retention can also lead to weight gain and a cough that is more pronounced at night or when lying down, indicating orthopnea. [MEDQUAD-03643] (Question 1)\n*   **MEDQUAD-04254**: Common signs of heart failure, such as shortness of breath, fatigue, and swelling in the ankles, feet, legs, abdomen, and neck veins, are all caused by fluid accumulation in the body. These symptoms tend to worsen as the heart's function declines, initially appearing after physical exertion. [MEDQUAD-04254] (Question 1)\n*   **MEDQUAD-03648**: Heart failure commonly presents with shortness of breath, tiredness, and swelling, which are all consequences of fluid buildup in the body. This fluid retention can manifest as weight gain, frequent urination, and coughing. [MEDQUAD-03648] (Question 1)\n*   **MEDQUAD-03653**: When diagnosing heart failure, a physical examination involves checking for abnormal heart and lung sounds that suggest fluid accumulation, as well as observing for visible signs of swelling. These findings help identify the presence of fluid buildup associated with heart failure. [MEDQUAD-03653] (Question 1)"}
