{"type": "tool_result", "t": 1791233178.759, "agent": "guideline-researcher", "name": "search_clinical_reference", "preview": "Found 6 hybrid matches from 'clinical_reference':\nIMPORTANT: Cite Doc IDs when using this information.\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.033)\n  Title: What is (are) Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\n  Content: Question: What is (are) Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\nAnswer: Keeping the proper level of potassium in the blood is essential. Potassium keeps the heart beating regularly and muscles working right. Problems can occur when blood potassium levels are either too low or too high. Damaged kidneys allow potassium to build up in the blood, causing serious heart problems. Potassium is found in many fruits and vegetables, such as bananas, potatoes, avocados, and melons. People...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-01688#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.030)\n  Title: What is (are) Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\n  Content: Question: What is (are) Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\nAnswer: CKD usually takes a long time to develop and does not go away. In CKD, the kidneys continue to workjust not as well as they should. Wastes may build up so gradually that the body becomes used to having those wastes in the blood. Salts containing phosphorus and potassium may rise to unsafe levels, causing heart and bone problems. Anemialow red blood cell countcan result from CKD because the kidneys stop maki...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-02311#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.030)\n  Title: What is (are) Nutrition for Early Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\n  Content: Question: What is (are) Nutrition for Early Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\nAnswer: CKD usually takes a long time to develop and does not go away. In CKD, the kidneys continue to workjust not as well as they should. Wastes may build up so gradually that the body becomes used to having those wastes in the blood. Salts containing phosphorus and potassium may rise to unsafe levels, causing heart and bone problems. Anemialow red blood cell countcan result from CKD because the kidneys stop making ...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-02342#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.030)\n  Title: What are the complications of Mineral and Bone Disorder in Chronic Kidney Disease ?\n  Content: Question: What are the complications of Mineral and Bone Disorder in Chronic Kidney Disease ?\nAnswer: The complications of mineral and bone disorder in CKD include slowed bone growth and deformities, and heart and blood vessel problems.\n\nSlowed Bone Growth and Deformities\n\nDamaged kidneys must work harder to clear phosphorus from the body. High levels of phosphorus cause lower levels of calcium in the blood, resulting in the following series of events:-When a persons blood calcium level becomes ...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-02312#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.030)\n  Title: Who is at risk for Nutrition for Early Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults? ?\n  Content: Question: Who is at risk for Nutrition for Early Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults? ?\nAnswer: Millions of Americans are at risk for developing CKD bec ..."}
{"type": "thought", "t": 1791233179.34, "agent": "evidence-researcher", "text": "Here are the PubMed evidence findings for Patient Y:\n\n*   **PMID-23622909**: Access to heart failure care post emergency department visit: do we meet established benchmarks and does it matter?\n    *   **Research Question**: Does access to heart failure care post-emergency department visit within established benchmarks impact outcomes?\n    *   **Design and Population**: This study investigated whether patients who had an ED visit for HF subsequently consult a physician within the Canadian Cardiology Society's recommended 2-week benchmark.\n    *   **Finding**: The study found that timely physician consultation after an ED visit for HF is important. Patients who did not receive timely follow-up may have worse outcomes.\n    *   **Bearing on Patient Y**: This suggests that ensuring Patient Y has a follow-up appointment within two weeks of discharge or an ED visit for heart failure could be crucial for preventing readmission or mortality."}
{"type": "tool_result", "t": 1791233180.043, "agent": "lead", "name": "task", "preview": "Here are the PubMed evidence findings for Patient Y:\n\n*   **PMID-23622909**: Access to heart failure care post emergency department visit: do we meet established benchmarks and does it matter?\n    *   **Research Question**: Does access to heart failure care post-emergency department visit within established benchmarks impact outcomes?\n    *   **Design and Population**: This study investigated whether patients who had an ED visit for HF subsequently consult a physician within the Canadian Cardiology Society's recommended 2-week benchmark.\n    *   **Finding**: The study found that timely physician consultation after an ED visit for HF is important. Patients who did not receive timely follow-up may have worse outcomes.\n    *   **Bearing on Patient Y**: This suggests that ensuring Patient Y has a follow-up appointment within two weeks of discharge or an ED visit for heart failure could be crucial for preventing readmission or mortality."}
{"type": "thought", "t": 1791233180.059, "agent": "guideline-researcher", "text": "*   **MEDQUAD-01695**: Maintaining proper potassium levels is crucial for heart rhythm and muscle function, but damaged kidneys in CKD can lead to potassium buildup in the blood. This hyperkalemia poses a serious risk for heart problems, highlighting the danger of high potassium in kidney disease. [MEDQUAD-01695] (Question 2)\n*   **MEDQUAD-01688**: In chronic kidney disease (CKD), wastes can accumulate gradually, and salts like phosphorus and potassium can reach unsafe levels. Elevated potassium can cause heart and bone problems, emphasizing the dangers of hyperkalemia in CKD. [MEDQUAD-01688] (Question 2)"}
