62:{"type": "tool_result", "t": 1791232817.004, "agent": "guideline-researcher", "name": "search_clinical_reference", "preview": "Found 6 hybrid matches from 'clinical_reference':\nIMPORTANT: Cite Doc IDs when using this information.\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-01695#chunk-0] (relevance: 0.032)\n  Title: What is (are) Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\n  Content: Question: What is (are) Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\nAnswer: Keeping the proper level of potassium in the blood is essential. Potassium keeps the heart beating regularly and muscles working right. Problems can occur when blood potassium levels are either too low or too high. Damaged kidneys allow potassium to build up in the blood, causing serious heart problems. Potassium is found in many fruits and vegetables, such as bananas, potatoes, avocados, and melons. People...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-05638#chunk-1] (relevance: 0.031)\n  Title: What is (are) hyperkalemic periodic paralysis ?\n  Content: Most people with hyperkalemic periodic paralysis have increased levels of potassium in their blood (hyperkalemia) during attacks. Hyperkalemia results when the weak or paralyzed muscles release potassium ions into the bloodstream. In other cases, attacks are associated with normal blood potassium levels (normokalemia). Ingesting potassium can trigger attacks in affected individuals, even if blood potassium levels do not go up.\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-01697#chunk-1] (relevance: 0.029)\n  Title: What to do for Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\n  Content: Most peoplewith or without CKDcan get the daily protein they need by eating two 3-ounce servings of meat or meat substitute.-People with CKD are at higher risk of having a heart attack or stroke.-Everyone needs dietary fat, but some fats are healthier than others.-Too much sodium in a persons diet can be harmful because it causes blood to hold fluid. People with CKD need to be careful not to let too much fluid build up in their bodies.-People with advanced CKD should avoid salt substitutes that ...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-02125#chunk-1] (relevance: 0.016)\n  Title: What are the treatments for Diabetic Kidney Disease ?\n  Content: Answer: People with diabetes should work with their health care team to prevent or manage CKD through the following steps:-measure A1C levelsa blood test that provides information about a persons average blood glucose levels for the previous 3 months at least twice a year and keep A1C levels below 7 percent-learn about insulin injections, diabetes medications, meal planning, physical activity, and blood glucose monitoring-find out whether protein, salt, or liquid should be limited in the diet-se...\n\n[Doc ID: MEDQUAD-01697#chunk-2] (relevance: 0.016)\n  Title: What to do for Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\n  Content: Potassium is found in many fruits and vegetables, such as bananas, potatoes, avocados, and melons.-Too much phosphorus in the blood pulls calcium from the bones, making the bones weak and likely to break.-People with advanced CKD may need to limit how much they drink becaus ..."}
63:{"type": "thought", "t": 1791232821.648, "agent": "guideline-researcher", "text": "The reference text does not directly address specific renal function and potassium monitoring/threshold cautions for ACE inhibitors and spironolactone/MRAs in the context of CKD and hyperkalemia. However, it does state that maintaining proper potassium levels is crucial for heart and muscle function, and damaged kidneys can lead to potassium buildup, causing serious heart problems [MEDQUAD-01695].\n\n**3) What HFrEF medication classes are recommended, including SGLT2 inhibitors if found?**\n\nThe reference text does not provide information on recommended HFrEF medication classes, including SGLT2 inhibitors.\n\n**4) What metformin cautions apply with CKD/eGFR ~42 and acute decompensated illness if found?**\n\nThe reference text does not provide information on metformin cautions with CKD/eGFR ~42 and acute decompensated illness.\n\n**5) What transition/self-care guidance is recommended for HF, including daily weights, taking diuretics as prescribed, low sodium, follow-up, and when to seek care?**\n\nThe reference text does not provide specific transition/self-care guidance for heart failure, including daily weights, taking diuretics as prescribed, low sodium, follow-up, and when to seek care.\n\n**Source List:**\n\n*   **MEDQUAD-04254:** What are the symptoms of Heart Failure ?\n*   **MEDQUAD-03643:** What are the symptoms of Heart Failure ?\n*   **MEDQUAD-03648:** What are the symptoms of Heart Failure ?\n*   **MEDQUAD-01695:** What is (are) Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?\n\nHere is a complete and concise report based on the clinical reference search for Patient Y:\n\n**1) What post-discharge symptoms/weight changes suggest worsening HF/congestion and need prompt clinical assessment?**\nCommon symptoms indicating worsening heart failure and congestion include shortness of breath, fatigue, and swelling in the ankles, feet, legs, and abdomen [MEDQUAD-04254, MEDQUAD-03643, MEDQUAD-03648]. These symptoms arise from fluid buildup in the body, which can also manifest as weight gain and a cough, particularly at night or when lying down [MEDQUAD-03643, MEDQUAD-03648]. As the heart's condition deteriorates, these signs become more pronounced, necessitating prompt clinical evaluation [MEDQUAD-04254].\n\n**2) What renal function and potassium monitoring/threshold cautions apply to ACE inhibitors and spironolactone/MRAs, especially with CKD/hyperkalemia?**\nThe reference text does not directly address specific renal function and potassium monitoring/threshold cautions for ACE inhibitors and spironolactone/MRAs in the context of CKD and hyperkalemia. However, it emphasizes that maintaining proper potassium levels is essential for regular heartbeats and muscle function, and damaged kidneys can lead to potassium accumulation in the blood, which can cause serious heart problems [MEDQUAD-01695].\n\n**3) What HFrEF medication classes are recommended, including SGLT2 inhibitors if found?**\nThe reference text does not provide information on recommended HFrEF medication classes, including SGLT2 inhibitors.\n\n**4) What metformin cautions apply with CKD/eGFR ~42 and acute decompensated illness if found?**\nThe reference text does not provide information on metformin cautions with CKD/eGFR ~42 and acute decompensated illness.\n\n**5) What transition/self-care guidance is recommended for HF, including daily weights, taking diuretics as prescribed, low sodium, follow-up, and when to seek care?**\nThe reference text does not provide specific transition/self-care guidance for heart failure, including daily weights, taking diuretics as prescribed, low sodium, follow-up, and when to seek care.\n\n**Source List:**\n*   **MEDQUAD-04254:** What are the symptoms of Heart Failure ?\n*   **MEDQUAD-03643:** What are the symptoms of Heart Failure ?\n*   **MEDQUAD-03648:** What are the symptoms of Heart Failure ?\n*   **MEDQUAD-01695:** What is (are) Nutrition for Advanced Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults ?"}
